hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in a short  lecture existentialism is a humanism  jean-paul Sartre brings up three terms  which he calls grand eloquent anguish  forlorn nest and despair or in the  original French on gwass navei Simone  and days is for each of these is a vital  characteristic you could say of the  human condition Sartre is is not saying  that they're actually part of human  nature because of course from the  existentialist point of view as he  articulates it human nature is something  that we ourselves determine it existence  precedes essence but each of these you  could say is a dimension of the  inescapable human condition so there is  something that does apply to all  humanity and he provides an explanation  of what he means by each of these terms  in the course of the lecture as he  actually says as you will see it's all  quite simple so he's suggesting that  anybody can grasp these so he says let's  take English first the existentialist  at once say it all together that man or  the human being is a  and another way of translating anguish  is anxiety this is coming from the onth  that is referenced by Heidegger anguish  anxiety these sort of things overlap  with 