ne so we're always  in anxiety we always have to make  decisions even if we choose not to make  a decision we're still making a decision  you can't get away from that until  you're dead that's that's the the basis  of human being is decision choosing  oftentimes without knowing the full  situation that you're choosing in right  so we either feel that anxiety or we can  steal it from ourselves we repress it we  bury it within he says anguish is  evident even when it does conceal itself  and he brings up the example of Abraham  he also talks about this woman who is  hearing hallucination someone would  speak to her on the telephone and give  her orders her doctor asked who is it  talk who's talking to you and she said  well it's God and then what proof that  SART says do I have that it is God if an  angel comes to me what proof is it that  it's an angel if I hear voices what  proof is there that they come from  heaven and not from Heller from the  subconscious or a pathological condition  I'm the one who chooses that  interpretation and I am responsible for  that choice so I can't get away from  that I can't get away from the anxiety  of having to choose over and over and  over again even if I make a commitment I  say okay I'm gonna make this choice once  and for all  I still have to keep making that  commitment or rather remaking that  commitment reach using every time that  there's a possibility for me to go  somewhere else with it  so we can't evade choices and the  choices that we make are with  indeterminate circumstances we don't  entirely determine the very condition  for our choosing he has the example  here a military leader who takes the  responsibility for an attack and sends a  certain number of men to their death he  chooses to do so and he alone makes the  choice for the most part orders come  from above but the orders are always too  broad he has to interpret them and he  says in making a decision he cannot help  feeling a certain anguish all leaders  know this anguish this does not keep  them from acting on the contrary it's  the very condition of their acting a lot  of people want to say well I'm not going  to make a decision until everything is  perfectly clear no you actually do have  to make a decision and you have to bear  the responsibility for it the next thing  that he talks about is forlorn Ness do  they sum all and what he means by this  is he formulates it in in one quick way  of expressing it there is no a priori  good when it comes to our  decision-making there is nothing that is  out there in the world or coming down to  us from on high or within ourselves  speaking you know with the still voice  of conscience or anything like that that  gives us an automatic here's your answer  there's nothing like that and the only  reason according to start why we think  that there is is because we convinced  ourselves of that we use our freedom to  tell ourselves that there's this thing  conditioning our freedom he frames it in  terms of we mean that God does not exist  and we have to face all the consequences  on this why bring up God well if God did  exist and gods  you know statements were clear of course  for many religious people the fact that  God exists does not clear up everything  and actually complicates things but  let's go with start on this if God did  exist then there would be some sort of  hierarchy of values or imperatives or  whatever it happens to be that we could  rely upon completely and follow out and  human beings would have a determinant  nature because they would be created by  God with that very nature  and we could examine it and figure it  out so you know this is a rejection of  not only divine command theories but  also natural law theories and many other  ethical theories as well so he's  rejecting religious ethics any sort of  ethics that would base itself upon God  said or God did or any anything  referencing God he's also rejecting a  lot of secular ethics and he talks about  a certain kind of secular ethics popular  in France in the 1800s also back in the  1700s to some extent among the  Enlightenment Phil assaults but  especially in the 1800's and going down  to starts own time that says that God is  a useless and costly hypothesis we're  going to discard it but in order for  there to be an ethics the society of  civilization it's essential certain  values be taken seriously and that they  be considered as having an OP priori  existence it must be obligatory a priori  that is before any experience the you  know sort of a universal condition for  humankind to be honest not to lie - not  to beat your wife to have children etc  etc and you know what what Sartre sees  this is doing is basically trying to  have your God cake have your not god  cake and eat it too right you're trying  to get the same sort of thing out  philosophy or science or some other  scheme that religious people got out of  it and start says no no we have to take  it seriously if there is no God then as  Dostoevsky says everything is permitted  we have to acknowledge that there's this  wide range of possibility and  variability and we don't get any sort of  criteria that we can rely upon as an  absolute starting point for ethics so he  says there's no fixed and given human  nature  that we could rely upon we can't look  within and as natural law theorists say  use reason to examine our impulses we  can't or you know inclinations so that  we can figure out well  life is to be protected we have to  educate our children we should learn the  truth no we those may be true but we  have to make them true we have to choose  them we can't just say oh well I've got  the answers now I need just to apply it  so no fixed and given human nature start  also said there is no determinism and  here this could easily be misunderstood  he doesn't mean that if I drop this book  it won't fall to the earth you know not  just nine times out of ten but  guaranteed or all the other things that  we think are deterministic but the human  being is constantly emerging out of the  term causal deterministic Nexus and then  you know using the their freedom and  coming back into it with the Nexus being  changed as a result so we can't claim  any sort of determinism whether it be an  individual one of well I've got the  genes that caused me to be this way or  my environment made me this way  we can't talk about it in terms of  larger social determinisms well I was  born into poverty so I had to behave  this way or I was born into you know the  house with the proverbial silver spoon  in my mouth as a rich person so I have  to see things this way start says no  those are cop-outs  we always do choose there is no  determinism that decides for us how  we're going to choose we choose to make  that determinism deterministic in our  choices we are the ones who say oh well  you know Freud says that I have this  complex or the CBT therapist says I have  to look at things this way or pick  whatever other psychological or  sociological or whatever other theory  you want were the ones who take that on  and decide to act that way he criticizes  as well the appeal to passion he says  the existentialist does not believe in  the power of passion he will never agree  that a sweeping passion is a ravaging  torrent which fatally leads a man to  certain acts and as therefore is an  excuse he thinks that the human being is  responsible for their passion so whether  that passion be that of revenge or that  of love that of  Saro that of anxiety it's what we do  with our emotions that decides our  actions not the emotions sweeping us  along and making us follow in their  course he says that sort of romanticism  or that sort of fatalism  is something that we choose it's not  something that we can take is an opera  re he also says there's no signs that we  could appeal to you know like a sign  from God or the universe or pick  whatever you want karma and some vague  you know new agey sense we are the ones  who choose to see them as signs we are  the ones to interpret them as signs  there's no getting away for that that is  why we are forlorn we don't have  anything that solves the question of  life and the questions of choices and  actions for us we have to choose so you  can see how for laurynas and anguish go  along together  the third condition that start talks  about is another condition for our  choices and actions and that is what he  calls despaired and it's a little bit  miss named right this sends a very  pessimistic message because of its  connotations both in the French days as  far and in the English despair but all  he means by despair is that we ourselves  are the ones who have to provide  everything we can count on the world to  some extent he talks about probabilities  he says we have to confine ourselves to  reckoning only with what depends on our  will or on the ensemble of probabilities  which make our action possible what do  we actually have any say in I won't even  say control over but say in what we  choose and then this penumbra of  possibilities that surround us they may  cooperate with us they may totally  overturn us so the existentialist can  say for example  you're out in the middle of the ocean  the boat goes down you manage to get  into one of the lifeboats and now the  lifeboat you know you're close to shore  but it's a pretty rough sea you know and  you're like well I think I better start  swimming right rather than just simply  cling to the lifeboat you choose to get  out there the waves may not cooperate  with you  you may die there's nothing that says oh  well if you just put it out there into  the universe the universe will respond  to you know the universe may crush you  down right or it could be other human  beings who are not going to cooperate  with you a great example of this is in  starts famous short story the wall you  know nobody there  is really able except for the narrating  character to evade their execution  because other people want to execute  them and the only thing that saves him  is a stroke of luck  all we can count on is ourselves and  then perhaps some cooperation from the  rest of the world as he says when we  want something he says we have to reckon  with probabilities  I may be counting for example on the  arrival of a friend the friend is coming  by rail or streetcar this supposes how  many different things the train arrives  on schedule the streetcar will not jump  the track the moment the possibilities I  am considering are not rigorously  involved by my action I ought to says  disengage myself from them because no  God no scheme can adapt the world and  its possibilities to my will then he  considers a response from one of the  main movements of the time the Marxist  who say well if you're doing the right  thing you can count on the workers the  proletariat to see your project further  and start says no you can't why not  because they are also free human beings  like myself they could choose next week  to all become fascist  that's the example that he uses you  can't count on a movement you can't  count on some sort of program you can't  count on anything to necessarily see  your action through and so does that  keep  from acting no of course not you still  have to act but you do it in despair  knowing that your action may be totally  ineffective knowing that other people  may misinterpret it because that is the  possibility for their choices so we have  these three fundamental dimensions or  characteristics of the human condition  being narrated here you see how all  three of them fit into each other  anguish for lorna and spare  you
 

